D={
"Krakatoa eruption":"After months of activity, Krakatoa, a volcanic island between Java and Sumatra, exploded on 27 August 1883 with a blast heard 4,800 km away on Rodrigues Island — the loudest sound in recorded history. Tsunamis up to 40 m high killed about 36,000 people on nearby coasts. Ash cooled global temperatures by about 1.2 °C and caused blood-red sunsets for months; telegraph cables spread the news worldwide within hours. A new island, Anak Krakatau ('child of Krakatoa'), emerged in 1927.",
"Antonine Plague":"Roman troops returning from the siege of Seleucia in Mesopotamia brought an epidemic around 165 CE, probably smallpox, which spread across the empire. The physician Galen described fever, diarrhoea and a black rash. It may have killed 10% of the population — 5 to 10 million people — including perhaps co-emperor Lucius Verus, and it recurred in 189 CE. Labour and army shortages weakened Marcus Aurelius's defence of the Danube frontier.",
"Cholera pandemics":"Cholera, a waterborne bacterial disease, had long been endemic in the Ganges delta. In 1817 it spread along British military and trade routes to Asia, the Middle East and Russia, then to Europe and the Americas in later waves; seven pandemics have occurred, the latest beginning in 1961. In 1854 John Snow traced a London outbreak to a contaminated water pump, and Robert Koch identified the bacterium in Calcutta in 1884. These discoveries led cities to build sewers and clean water supplies.",
"Third plague pandemic":"Beginning in Yunnan, China in the 1850s, bubonic plague reached Hong Kong in 1894 and Bombay in 1896 via shipping. India suffered about 12 million deaths of the global 15 million. Alexandre Yersin and Kitasato Shibasaburō identified the plague bacterium in Hong Kong. Harsh British measures in Pune — house searches and forced segregation — led the Chapekar brothers to assassinate plague commissioner W. C. Rand in 1897.",
"Spanish flu":"A deadly H1N1 influenza strain spread in three waves from 1918 to 1920, carried by troopships at the end of World War I. It infected about a third of the world's population and killed perhaps 50 million, unusually striking healthy young adults. India lost an estimated 12–17 million, more than any other country, as the virus arrived via Bombay with returning soldiers during a drought. It was called 'Spanish' only because neutral Spain's newspapers reported it freely.",
"HIV/AIDS pandemic":"HIV-1 crossed from chimpanzees to humans in southeastern Cameroon, probably through bushmeat hunting, and spread via colonial-era Kinshasa in the early 20th century. AIDS was recognised in 1981 and HIV identified in 1983. About 40 million people have died, and sub-Saharan Africa was hit hardest. Antiretroviral therapy since 1996, and later cheaper Indian generic drugs, turned it into a manageable chronic condition.",
"COVID-19 pandemic":"A new coronavirus, SARS-CoV-2, was identified in Wuhan, China, in December 2019 and declared a pandemic on 11 March 2020. Lockdowns closed schools and borders worldwide; India's 2021 Delta wave overwhelmed hospitals and oxygen supplies. mRNA and other vaccines were developed within a year, and India produced Covishield and Covaxin. WHO estimates over 7 million recorded deaths, with excess deaths perhaps closer to 15–20 million.",
"Buddha's enlightenment":"Prince Siddhartha Gautama of the Shakya clan, born at Lumbini, renounced his comfortable life after seeing old age, sickness and death. After six years of extreme austerity, he chose a 'middle way' and meditated under a pipal tree at Bodh Gaya until he attained enlightenment, becoming the Buddha ('awakened one'), probably in the 5th century BCE. He gave his first sermon at Sarnath on the Four Noble Truths, and taught for 45 years across the Ganga plain.",
"Kalinga War":"Kalinga, on India's east coast, controlled sea trade to Southeast Asia and had resisted Mauryan rule. About 261 BCE Ashoka conquered it. His own Rock Edict XIII records that 150,000 people were deported, 100,000 killed and many more died of famine and disease. Ashoka's remorse turned him toward Buddhism and dhamma; he gave up wars of conquest in favour of 'conquest by righteousness'.",
"Unification of China under Qin":"Between 230 and 221 BCE, King Ying Zheng of Qin, using Legalist reforms that made Qin's farmers and soldiers efficient and disciplined, conquered the six other Warring States one by one: Han, Zhao, Wei, Chu, Yan and Qi. He proclaimed himself Qin Shi Huang, the First Emperor. He standardised writing, coins, laws and axle widths and created commanderies run by appointed officials, which shaped Chinese government for two millennia.",
"Punic Wars":"Rome and Carthage fought three wars over the western Mediterranean. In the First (264–241 BCE) Rome built a navy and won Sicily; in the Second (218–201 BCE) Hannibal crossed the Alps with elephants and destroyed Roman armies at Lake Trasimene and Cannae, but Scipio invaded Africa and defeated him at Zama. In the Third (149–146 BCE) Rome besieged and destroyed Carthage, selling its survivors into slavery.",
"Roman conquest of Gaul":"Julius Caesar, governor of Gaul from 58 BCE, used conflicts among Gallic tribes to intervene, defeating the Helvetii, Belgae and Veneti and crossing to Britain and the Rhine. In 52 BCE the Arverni chief Vercingetorix united the Gauls; Caesar besieged him at Alesia and defeated a relief army. Plutarch claims a million Gauls died and a million were enslaved. Gaul became Romanised, and French developed from Latin.",
"Roman conquest of Britain":"Emperor Claudius invaded in 43 CE with four legions, seeking glory. Boudica of the Iceni revolted in 60–61 CE after Roman abuses, burning Camulodunum, Londinium and Verulamium before being defeated. Hadrian's Wall (from 122 CE) marked the northern frontier. Rome built roads, towns and villas, but withdrew around 410 as the empire weakened.",
"Assassination of Julius Caesar":"After winning the civil war, Caesar was named dictator for life in 44 BCE, alarming senators who feared monarchy. On the Ides of March, about 60 conspirators led by Brutus and Cassius stabbed him 23 times at a Senate meeting in the Theatre of Pompey. Instead of restoring the Republic, his death led to civil wars between the conspirators and Antony and Octavian, ending in Augustus's empire.",
"Crucifixion of Jesus":"Jesus, a Jewish preacher from Galilee, came to Jerusalem at Passover around 30–33 CE. Temple authorities saw him as a threat, and the Roman prefect Pontius Pilate had him crucified, a punishment for rebels. His followers proclaimed that he rose from the dead; the apostle Paul and others spread the faith across the Roman world, and Christianity became the empire's religion by the late 4th century.",
"Hijra and rise of Islam":"Muhammad began preaching in Mecca around 610, facing persecution from the Quraysh elite. In 622 he and his followers migrated to Yathrib (Medina), where he led a community governed by the Constitution of Medina — the year 1 of the Islamic calendar. After battles at Badr, Uhud and the Trench, Mecca surrendered in 630, and by his death in 632 most of Arabia had accepted Islam.",
"Arab conquest of Sindh":"After pirates seized ships carrying gifts to the Umayyad governor al-Hajjaj, he sent his 17-year-old nephew Muhammad bin Qasim to Sindh. In 712 Qasim took the port of Debal with catapults and defeated King Dahir at Aror. Sindh became an Umayyad province, Hindus and Buddhists were treated as protected people paying jizya, and Arab scholars learned Indian mathematics and astronomy.",
"Battle of Tours":"After conquering Spain from 711, Umayyad armies raided into Gaul. In October 732 Abd al-Rahman al-Ghafiqi's army met Charles Martel's Franks between Tours and Poitiers; the Frankish infantry formed a solid square and Abd al-Rahman was killed. The Arabs withdrew, and Charles's family, the Carolingians, rose to power.",
"Battle of Talas":"In 751 a Tang army under the Korean-born general Gao Xianzhi fought an Abbasid army near the Talas river in Kyrgyzstan. The Karluk Turks switched sides, and the Tang lost. It ended Chinese expansion into Central Asia, and tradition says captured Chinese papermakers taught the craft in Samarkand.",
"Chola naval expedition to Srivijaya":"Srivijaya controlled the straits through which Chola merchants traded with Song China. In 1025 Rajendra I sent a fleet across the Bay of Bengal that captured Srivijaya's king and raided ports including Kadaram (Kedah) and Palembang, as recorded in the Tanjore inscription. It secured Chola commerce, making it a rare example of Indian overseas military power.",
"Third Battle of Panipat":"The Marathas, controlling much of India, clashed with Afghan ruler Ahmad Shah Durrani, allied with Najib-ud-Daula and Shuja-ud-Daula. After months of standoff, the hungry Maratha army under Sadashivrao Bhau attacked on 14 January 1761 and was annihilated; Vishwasrao and Bhau died. Perhaps 100,000 died including civilians. It halted Maratha expansion and left a power vacuum the British exploited.",
"Battle of Buxar":"After Mir Qasim, the Nawab of Bengal, tried to curb Company privileges, he allied with Shuja-ud-Daula of Awadh and Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II. On 22 October 1764 Hector Munro's Company army of about 7,000 defeated them. The resulting Treaty of Allahabad (1765) gave the Company the diwani of Bengal, Bihar and Odisha.",
"Great Bengal Famine":"In 1769–70 a failed monsoon struck Bengal and Bihar. The Company, newly in charge of revenue, kept collecting taxes and its officials hoarded rice. About 10 million people — a third of Bengal's population — died, and farmland went back to jungle. It is a classic example of colonial famine policy.",
"American Revolution":"After the Seven Years' War, Britain taxed its American colonies (Stamp Act, Tea Act), provoking protests like the Boston Tea Party (1773). Fighting began at Lexington and Concord in 1775; the Declaration of Independence (4 July 1776), written by Thomas Jefferson, declared that 'all men are created equal'. With French help, George Washington won at Yorktown (1781), and the US Constitution (1787) created a federal republic.",
"French Revolution":"France was bankrupt from wars, and bread prices were high. In 1789 the Third Estate formed a National Assembly, crowds stormed the Bastille, and feudal privileges were abolished. The monarchy fell in 1792; Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette were guillotined, and Robespierre's Terror executed about 17,000. Napoleon seized power in 1799, but the ideals of liberty, equality and fraternity spread worldwide.",
"Haitian Revolution":"Saint-Domingue was the world's richest colony, producing sugar with about 500,000 enslaved Africans. In August 1791 a revolt began after the Bois Caïman ceremony; Toussaint Louverture became its leader, defeating Spanish and British forces. Napoleon tried to restore slavery and captured Toussaint, but Jean-Jacques Dessalines defeated the French, and Haiti declared independence on 1 January 1804.",
"Napoleonic Wars":"Napoleon, crowned emperor in 1804, won brilliant victories at Austerlitz (1805), Jena (1806) and Wagram (1809), dominating Europe and spreading the Napoleonic Code. The British navy won at Trafalgar (1805), and the Peninsular War drained France. His invasion of Russia in 1812 lost most of his 600,000-man army. Defeated at Leipzig (1813), he returned from Elba and lost at Waterloo (1815).",
"Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki":"The Manhattan Project built atomic bombs in the US. On 6 August 1945 'Little Boy' destroyed Hiroshima, and on 9 August 'Fat Man' struck Nagasaki; about 110,000–210,000 people died by the end of 1945. With the Soviet Union also declaring war on Japan, Emperor Hirohito announced surrender on 15 August. The bombings began the nuclear age.",
"Indian Independence and Partition":"Weakened by World War II and facing the naval mutiny of 1946, Britain decided to leave India. Mountbatten advanced the date to August 1947, and the Radcliffe Line split Punjab and Bengal. India and Pakistan became independent on 14–15 August. About 15 million people crossed the new borders and up to 1–2 million died in communal violence. The princely states had to choose sides, leading to conflict in Kashmir.",
"Homo sapiens at Jebel Irhoud":"Fossils from Jebel Irhoud, a cave in Morocco, were long thought to be about 40,000 years old. In 2017 new dating of burnt flints put them at about 315,000 years, making them the oldest known Homo sapiens. They had modern faces but elongated braincases, suggesting our species evolved across Africa over a long time.",
"Blombos Cave engravings":"Blombos Cave on South Africa's southern coast contains ochre pieces engraved with cross-hatched patterns about 75,000–100,000 years old, shell beads and a 100,000-year-old paint workshop with abalone shells. A 73,000-year-old drawing in red ochre on a stone flake is the oldest known drawing. They show early symbolic and planning abilities.",
"Arrival in Australia":"Modern humans crossed at least 90 km of open sea from Southeast Asia to reach Sahul (Australia and New Guinea joined by low sea levels), probably by raft or boat. The Madjedbebe rock shelter suggests arrival about 65,000 years ago, though some argue about 50,000. Aboriginal Australians have continuous cultures, with art, songlines and fire management of landscapes.",
"Oldest figurative cave art (Sulawesi)":"In Leang Karampuang cave on Sulawesi, Indonesia, a painting of three human-like figures interacting with a wild pig was dated in 2024 to at least 51,200 years ago, using uranium-series dating. It is the oldest known figurative and narrative art, showing that storytelling art emerged in Asia, not only Europe.",
"Natufian culture and first villages":"Between about 15,000 and 11,500 years ago, the Natufians of the Levant lived in villages of round stone houses year-round, harvesting wild wheat and barley with flint sickles and grinding stones. They buried their dead with ornaments and kept dogs. Their settled life and cereal use paved the way for farming.",
}
